// Regression test: `&&`/`||` between non-numeric, incompatible operands
// (here two different struct types) must be rejected with a real, readable
// error message.
//
// This was already rejected before the fix, but via a confusing path:
// TypeChecker::inferRelationalType()'s "logical operators" check tested for
// the literal characters '&'/'|' (the BITWISE operator tokens) instead of
// TOK_AND/TOK_OR (the real && / || tokens) -- a check that could never
// actually match, since visitBinary() routes bitwise '&'/'|' to a separate
// code path before this function is ever called. The dead check meant a
// genuine && / || silently fell through to unrelated "ordering operator"
// logic, which for a genuinely invalid case formatted the operator into the
// error message via `std::string(1, static_cast<char>(Op))` -- and TOK_AND/
// TOK_OR's values (-20/-21) are not printable ASCII when cast to char, so
// the message contained a garbage character instead of "&&"/"||". This test
// doesn't assert on the message text (the test runner only checks
// compile-fail status), but it does exercise the fixed code path directly.

struct A { x: int }
struct B { y: int }

fn main(): int {
    mut a: A = A::default();
    mut b: B = B::default();
    mut result: bool = a && b;
    return 0;
}
